\item \strong{Record-level habitat.} The records path labels every point
from the community recorded there (\code{TaxaHabitat::assign_habitat_biological()})
and keeps only points in \code{site_habitat}. Counts have no points. The
default, \code{habitat_filter = "none"}, applies no habitat restriction to
the counts and leaves \code{observed_in_habitat} \code{NA}.
\code{"taxon_threshold"} applies habitat per TAXON instead: a species
enters the composition when its \code{habitat_lookup} weight for
\code{site_habitat} is at least \code{habitat_threshold} -- numerator and
denominator alike. Neither is the records path's stratum. The records path keeps a species
whenever its records sit at points labelled \code{site_habitat}, so at a
coastal site it keeps shoreline terrestrial plants whose own habitat
weight is zero; a taxon-level filter drops them. On PtConception 18S
the taxon filter lost 50 of 377 species with \code{theta >= 1e-4} in the
record-level Marine stratum, while the unfiltered fit lost none (it
adds taxa the records path excludes instead, which only matters for
